    "0c2f9abc35db22bbf67c1140ed50c3d8445eba4d": {
      "message": "chore(release): make a version bump without a tag impossible\n\nPublishing is tag-triggered, but bumping `package.json` and tagging were\nseparate manual steps. Skip the second one and the version lives only in the\nmanifest: dsh-git reached npm as 0.2.0 → 0.5.0 while 0.3.0 and 0.4.0 were\nnever published, which is exactly the kind of gap a registry should not show.\n\nThree pieces, so the rule is enforced rather than remembered:\n\n- `scripts/check-release.mjs` asserts, for every package, that the manifest\n  version is tagged at a commit which declares the same version and is an\n  ancestor of HEAD. Offline on purpose: publishing only happens from tags, so\n  asking npm would just make the gate flaky. Covered by five tests against\n  throwaway repositories.\n- `release.mjs --bump <patch|minor|major>` does the whole release in one\n  command: bump, rebuild the browser half (its banner carries the version),\n  rewrite the install pins, commit `release: <dir> <version>`, tag, and with\n  `--push` push both refs in a single push. `--dry-run` prints the plan.\n- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs the gate on every push to `release` and on\n  pull requests. `publish.yml` now refuses a tag older than npm's `latest`,\n  so a stray old tag cannot move the dist-tag backwards.\n\nAGENTS.md records the conventions this repository actually follows: artifacts\nthat must be committed and rebuilt, the no-hand-edited-version rule, bilingual\ndocs, portal data sync, dependency discipline, and what not to touch.\n\nThe dsh-git README now anchors the tool removal on 0.5.0 — the first published\nversion after 0.2.0 — instead of the unpublished 0.3.0.",
